---
/**
 * Why a beta-blocker breaks the heart-rate bands, and the 1-to-10 scale used
 * instead. Rendered by /science/movement/effort; see scienceTopics.ts.
 */
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import { EFFORT_SECTION } from "../../data/science";
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";
const zone2 = topicHref("zone-2");
const volume = topicHref("weekly-volume");
const workout = topicHref("a-workout");

/** Apple's four words, Verve's three bands, and the ACSM scales they line up with. */
const mapping: Array<[string, string, string, string, string]> = [
  ["1–3", "Easy", "light", "under 3", "under 12"],
  ["4–6", "Moderate", "moderate", "3–4", "12–13"],
  ["7–8", "Hard", "vigorous", "5 and up", "14 and up"],
  ["9–10", "All out", "vigorous", "5 and up", "14 and up"],
];
---

<div class="mt-[18px] rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <p class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-text-tertiary">
    One rating, three bands
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] text-[12.5px]">
      <thead>
        <tr class="text-left font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-text-tertiary">
          <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Apple 1–10</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Apple's word</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Verve counts it as</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Borg CR10</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Borg RPE 6–20</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/[0.06]">
        {mapping.map(([a, w, v, cr, rpe]) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono font-semibold text-text-primary">{a}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-text-secondary">{w}</td>
            <td class={`px-3 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider ${v === "vigorous" ? "text-danger" : v === "moderate" ? "text-met-hero" : "text-success-light"}`}>{v}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-text-secondary">{cr}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-text-secondary">{rpe}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-3 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    The two right-hand columns are the ACSM cut-points: CR10 3–4 or RPE
    12–13 is moderate, CR10 5 and above or RPE 14 and above is vigorous.
    Apple&rsquo;s four words are mapped onto them; nothing finer than that is
    claimed.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    Everything on the <a href={workout}>workout page</a> that says how hard a
    session was is read from heart rate: the three bands, the Zone 2 minutes,
    the moderate and vigorous minutes that climb the Weekly Ascent. For most
    people that is the right instrument. For a person on a beta-blocker it is
    the wrong one, and this page is about what Verve does instead.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What a beta-blocker does to the number.</strong>
    Beta-blockers blunt the heart-rate response to exertion: at a given
    workload the rate runs roughly 20 to 30 bpm lower than it would
    unmedicated, and the peak rate is held down the same way. The effort is
    real and the body is working as hard; the heart is simply not
    reporting it. So every band drawn from heart rate undercounts. A walk
    that would have sat in the middle of the moderate band reads as light;
    an interval session that would have been vigorous reads as moderate;
    the Zone 2 minutes are a fraction of the real ones. Re-drawing the bands
    lower does not fix it, because the blunting is not a fixed offset and
    the dose changes. The ACSM guideline&rsquo;s answer is the one Verve
    follows: on rate-limiting medication, prescribe and judge intensity by
    perceived exertion, not by heart rate.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">The 1-to-10 scale.</strong>
    From watchOS 11 and iOS 18, Apple Watch asks for an effort rating when
    a workout ends: 1 to 10, with Easy, Moderate, Hard and All out as the
    four words along it. The rating is stored in Apple Health as the Workout
    Effort Score, and for a session that was not rated Apple can store an
    estimated score of its own. Verve reads the rating as it is, with no
    smoothing and no re-scoring from heart rate, and the card says which
    kind it was: rated by you, or estimated. A watch that does not ask, or
    an older one, gets a row on the workout page where the rating can be
    typed. The scale is Borg&rsquo;s: the perceived strain tracks the
    physiological strain closely enough to stand in for it, which is the
    whole reason a number typed after the session can be a measure.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">How the rating becomes minutes.</strong>
    One effort per session. A 32-minute walk rated 6 is 32 moderate
    minutes; rated 8, it is 32 vigorous minutes. Heart rate can grade a
    session minute by minute, and this cannot: there is no warm-up
    discounted, no hard middle picked out, no cool-down trimmed. It is
    coarser, and honest about being coarser.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What switches over.</strong>
    When the profile says beta-blocker, the minutes, the <a href={zone2}>Zone
    2</a> total and the <a href={volume}>Weekly Ascent</a> are counted by
    effort instead of heart rate: a session rated 4 to 6 is a Zone 2 session
    in full, 7 and above climbs the Ascent as vigorous. The heart-rate trace
    is still drawn on the workout page, because it is what the watch
    measured, but nothing is graded from it. Strength, flexibility and
    balance are not affected: they were never graded by heart rate, and
    they still are not.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What does not switch over.</strong>
    The resting heart rate tile, one-minute recovery and night HRV are read
    against your own band as before. A beta-blocker lowers all three
    baselines, and the band moves with them, which is the point of a band
    that is yours. What the medication changes is the response to effort,
    and that is the only place the heart-rate reading has been retired.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {EFFORT_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
</div>
